Day 250/250: Minimum Cost Conversion II

Problem Statement #2977

Problem Statement Explantion:

You are given two strings, source and target, both of length n. 
You are also given two string arrays, original and changed, and an integer array cost, where cost[i] represents the cost of converting the string original[i] to changed[i].
You can perform any number of conversions. 
Each conversion involves replacing a non-overlapping substring of source that matches original[i] with changed[i]. 
The goal is to find the minimum cost to transform source into target. If it is impossible, return -1.

Solution Approch:

The problem is a combination of Graph Theory (Shortest Path) and Dynamic Programming.

1. Mapping Strings to Nodes
Since the transformations are based on strings rather than single characters, we first collect all unique strings from the original and changed arrays and map them to unique integer IDs. 
This allows us to represent the transformation rules as a graph.

2. All-Pairs Shortest Path (Floyd-Warshall)
Multiple transformations can be chained together (e.g., A→B and B→C). 
To find the minimum cost to convert any string U to string V:
We initialize a distance matrix with the given cost array.
We use the Floyd-Warshall algorithm to calculate the shortest path between all pairs of string IDs.

3. Dynamic Programming
We define dp[i] as the minimum cost to convert the prefix source[0...i-1] to target[0...i-1].
Base Case: dp[0] = 0.
Transition 1 (Match): If source[i] == target[i], then dp[i+1] = min(dp[i+1], dp[i]).
Transition 2 (Transformation): For every possible length L of strings in our transformation set, we check if the substring source[i...i+L] can be converted to target[i...i+L] using our precomputed shortest paths.

Here's the code:

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public long minimumCost(String source, String target, String[] original, String[] changed, int[] cost) {
        int n = source.length();
        Map<String, Integer> strToId = new HashMap<>();
        int idCounter = 0;
        
        for (String s : original) if (!strToId.containsKey(s)) strToId.put(s, idCounter++);
        for (String s : changed) if (!strToId.containsKey(s)) strToId.put(s, idCounter++);
        
        long[][] dist = new long[idCounter][idCounter];
        for (long[] row : dist) Arrays.fill(row, Long.MAX_VALUE / 2);
        for (int i = 0; i < idCounter; i++) dist[i][i] = 0;
        
        for (int i = 0; i < original.length; i++) {
            int u = strToId.get(original[i]);
            int v = strToId.get(changed[i]);
            dist[u][v] = Math.min(dist[u][v], (long) cost[i]);
        }
        
        for (int k = 0; k < idCounter; k++) {
            for (int i = 0; i < idCounter; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j < idCounter; j++) {
                    dist[i][j] = Math.min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]);
                }
            }
        }
        
        long[] dp = new long[n + 1];
        Arrays.fill(dp, Long.MAX_VALUE / 2);
        dp[0] = 0;
        
        Set<Integer> lengths = new HashSet<>();
        for (String s : original) lengths.add(s.length());
        
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (dp[i] == Long.MAX_VALUE / 2) continue;
            
            if (source.charAt(i) == target.charAt(i)) {
                dp[i + 1] = Math.min(dp[i + 1], dp[i]);
            }
            
            for (int len : lengths) {
                if (i + len <= n) {
                    String subS = source.substring(i, i + len);
                    String subT = target.substring(i, i + len);
                    
                    if (strToId.containsKey(subS) && strToId.containsKey(subT)) {
                        int u = strToId.get(subS);
                        int v = strToId.get(subT);
                        if (dist[u][v] < Long.MAX_VALUE / 2) {
                            dp[i + len] = Math.min(dp[i + len], dp[i] + dist[u][v]);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        
        return dp[n] >= Long.MAX_VALUE / 2 ? -1 : dp[n];
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(M^3+N⋅L)
Space Complexity: O(M^2+N)



